//// Decision 63 (amended 2026-09-19) — `xs[i]` is `xs.at(i)` through the ambient
//// `Index<i32, T>`, and its value is `T`. An index past the end has no `T` to
//// answer, so the program **fails** there (`index_past_the_end_fails.exit` says
//// `nonzero`): not `undefined`, not `null`, not `0`. `xs.at(9)` — the method
//// called by name — is the different feature that answers `?T` (decision 30,
//// `run/index_at_optional.bp`).
////
//// This is the third spelling of the cell C-16 names as
//// `index_an_index_past_the_end_answers_zero`: the slug said `zero`, the text
//// said `undefined`, and decision 63 makes the answer a failure on every backend.
////
//// Measured at `85f883bd` (2026-09-20), the compiler half (C-02) not landed:
////
////   commonJS  `10`, `undefined`, `after` — exit 0, the hole TypeScript leaves open
////   erlang    `10`, `undefined`, `after` — exit 0, the same
////   wasm      `10`, `0`, `after`         — exit 0, `$__arr_at` answers 0 out of range
////   beam      exit 0; the index is dropped rather than emitted (03 handover 15)

pub fn main() {
    val xs = [10, 20, 30];
    @print(xs[0]);
    @print(xs[9]);
    @print("after");
}
